    },
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "SEC-01",
        "text": "Start with the working tree: these are the checked-out files you edit. Changes here are not yet the next commit. Think of this as your active workspace, where you modify files before deciding what belongs in the next saved revision.",
        "claim_refs": [
          "CLAIM-001"
        ],
        "target_duration_seconds": 22.0
      },
      {
        "id": "SEC-02",
        "text": "When you run git add, Git updates the index with the selected content. The index is often called the staging area because it represents what you are proposing for the next commit. You can keep editing the working tree after staging, so the two can differ.",
        "claim_refs": [
          "CLAIM-002"
        ],
        "target_duration_seconds": 25.0
      },
      {
        "id": "SEC-03",
        "text": "Finally, git commit records the current contents of the index as a new commit. The sequence is simple: edit in the working tree, stage selected content into the index, then commit the index. Understanding these three states makes everyday Git much easier to reason about.",
        "claim_refs": [
          "CLAIM-003"
        ],
        "target_duration_seconds": 25.0
      }
    ],
